rn how to read short poems carefully, you'll expand and deepen your understanding of the Russian language, and you'll gain insight into one of the world's major literary traditions.\u00a0 Reading is in Russian (poems) and English (essays and critical works), discussion in English.\u00a0 The course work will be adjusted to the language proficiency of the class.\u00a0 Satisfies the Russian minor requirement for Russian literature with reading in the original.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, LA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "Reading is in Russian (poems) and English (essays and critical works), discussion in English.\u00a0The course work will be adjusted to the language proficiency of the class.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "Prerequisite: proficiency in Russian or permission of instructor.", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "Satisfies the Russian minor requirement for Russian literature with reading in the original.", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2853, "crseId": 373367, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "RUSSL", "catalogNbr": "4435", "titleShort": "Art, Nature, and Empire", "titleLong": "Art, Nature, and Empire: The Environment in Russian and Soviet Culture", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 19076, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:25AM", "startDt": "08/21/2023", "endDt": "12/04/2023", "instructors": [], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "White Hall B02", "bldgDescr": "White Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "WHTB02", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/21/2023", "endDt": "12/16/2023", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "COML", "catalogNbr": "4435", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "HIST", "catalogNbr": "4435", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/21/2023", "sessionEndDt": "12/16/2023", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "How does the state draw political power from nature? What is the relationship between the environment and national and/or imperial identity? How does the environment resist political control, or support human resistance? This course will explore these questions from the perspective of Russian and Soviet culture. Analyzing literature, art, and film in historical context, we will consider the environment as worker and victim, refuge and rebel, commodity and national(ist) emblem, exploring the degrees of agency it is granted in different artistic depictions. With special attention to the history of Russian imperialism and Soviet \"internal colonization\" and to non-Russian writers and artists of the Russian Empire and USSR, including Indigenous writers.", "catalogBreadth": "(HB)", "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, HST-AS, LA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "All readings will be in English.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", 